<body>
<header>
  <h1>BIEK Computer Science 12th Class Past Paper Analysis (2015–2025)</h1>
  <p>A data-driven trend analysis of HSC Part-2 Computer Science Paper-II question volume, question-type composition, and recurring content patterns for educators and curriculum planners.</p>
</header>
<main>
  <div class="kpi-grid" aria-label="Key performance indicators">
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Total rows analyzed</div><div class="value">{len(df)}</div><div class="note">Questions in data.csv</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Observed years</div><div class="value">{len(observed_years)}</div><div class="note">2015–2025, excluding {', '.join(map(str, missing_years))}</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Average per observed year</div><div class="value">{avg_per_type['Total']:.1f}</div><div class="note">Median: {median_total:.0f}</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Largest observed annual total</div><div class="value">{int(counts['Total'].max())}</div><div class="note">2017–2019</div></div>
  </div>

  <section id="overview">
    <h2>1. Dataset & Examination Overview</h2>
    <p>The dataset contains <strong>{len(df)} question rows</strong> with four fields: <code>id</code>, <code>year</code>, <code>type</code>, and <code>question</code>. It covers observed examination years from <strong>{min(observed_years)} to {max(observed_years)}</strong>, but <strong>no 2020 records appear in data.csv</strong>. All statistics in this report are calculated only from the provided CSV.</p>
    <div class="callout warning"><strong>Data integrity note:</strong> the source brief describes an 11-year span, but the file itself contains rows for {len(observed_years)} distinct years. The year 2020 is therefore treated as missing, not as a zero-question exam year.</div>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div>
        <h3>Overall Question-Type Breakdown</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Question type</th><th>Count</th><th>Share of dataset</th><th>Mean per observed year</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {''.join(f'<tr><td><span style="color:{colors[t]};font-weight:700">{t}</span></td><td>{int(overall_counts[t])}</td><td>{overall_pct[t]:.1f}%</td><td>{avg_per_type[t]:.1f}</td></tr>' for t in type_order)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What the structure implies</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>MCQs are fixed at 15 rows</strong> in every observed year.</li>
          <li><strong>Long questions are fixed at 3 rows</strong> in every observed year.</li>
          <li><strong>Short questions drive all volume variation</strong>, ranging from 9 to 18 rows.</li>
          <li>The overall composition is nearly balanced between MCQs ({overall_pct['MCQs']:.1f}%) and Short questions ({overall_pct['Short']:.1f}%), with Long questions at {overall_pct['Long']:.1f}%.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="yearly-distribution">
    <h2>2. Year-by-Year Question Volume Analysis</h2>
    <p>Question volume is not fully flat across the observed years. The exam moves from <strong>27 questions</strong> in 2015–2016 to <strong>36 questions</strong> in 2017–2019, then settles at <strong>33 questions</strong> from 2021–2025. Because 2020 is absent, the change from 2019 to 2021 is an <em>observed-year comparison</em>, not a confirmed year-over-year transition.</p>
    <div class="legend"><span style="--c:#111827">Total</span><span style="--c:{colors['MCQs']}">MCQs</span><span style="--c:{colors['Short']}">Short</span><span style="--c:{colors['Long']}">Long</span></div>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{svg_line_chart(series_map, all_years, y_label='Question count')}</div>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{svg_stacked_counts(all_years)}</div>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>MCQs</th><th>Short</th><th>Long</th><th>Total</th><th>Observed change</th><th>Observed change %</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{''.join(year_rows)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Volume finding:</strong> the only observed increase is from 2016 to 2017, rising by 9 questions (+33.3%). The only observed decline is from 2019 to 2021, falling by 3 questions (-8.3%), with 2020 missing from the dataset.</div>
  </section>

  <section id="question-type-analysis">
    <h2>3. Question Type Composition & Trends</h2>
    <p>The exam’s backbone is stable for MCQs and Long questions. The meaningful structural shift is the Short-question block: it doubles from <strong>9 rows</strong> in 2015–2016 to <strong>18 rows</strong> in 2017–2019, then moderates to <strong>15 rows</strong> in 2021–2025.</p>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{svg_100pct(all_years)}</div>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div>
        <h3>Annual Type Ratios</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>MCQs</th><th>Short</th><th>Long</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{''.join(type_ratio_rows)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Interpretation for curriculum planning</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>In 2015–2016, MCQs dominate the paper composition at <strong>55.6%</strong>.</li>
          <li>In 2017–2019, Short questions become the largest component at <strong>50.0%</strong>.</li>
          <li>From 2021–2025, MCQs and Short questions are balanced at <strong>45.5%</strong> each.</li>
          <li>Long questions remain a small but consistent requirement, ranging from <strong>8.3%</strong> to <strong>11.1%</strong> depending on total volume.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="topic-patterns">
    <h2>4. Recurring Topics & Question Content Patterns</h2>
    <p>Topic analysis was performed by normalizing the <code>question</code> text, replacing the <code>$</code> delimiter with spaces, filtering common stop words, and counting recurring terms and domain-specific keyword groups. This is a keyword-based content signal, not a manually coded syllabus taxonomy.</p>
    <h3>Highest-Frequency Terms</h3>
    <div class="chips">{term_chips}</div>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{svg_horizontal_bars(top_terms, height=500)}</div>
    <h3>Recurring Phrases</h3>
    <div class="chips">{bigram_chips}</div>
    <p class="small">Repeated phrases such as <strong>primary key</strong>, <strong>foreign key</strong>, <strong>format specifier</strong>, <strong>switch statement</strong>, and <strong>database model</strong> point to persistent database and programming assessment patterns.</p>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div>
        <h3>Topic Group Prevalence</h3>
        <div class="chart-wrap">{svg_concept_bars(concept_rows)}</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Topic Group Table</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Topic group</th><th>Question rows</th><th>Keyword mentions</th><th>Dataset share</th><th>Rows by type</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{''.join(concept_table)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <h3>Topic Signals by Year</h3>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{svg_heatmap(concept_year_counts)}</div>
    <div class="callout good"><strong>Content finding:</strong> C/C++ programming appears in {concept_rows[0][1]} question rows ({concept_rows[0][3]:.1f}% of the dataset), while database-system terms appear in {concept_rows[1][1]} rows ({concept_rows[1][3]:.1f}%). These two domains are the most consistent recurring areas in the supplied papers.</div>
  </section>

  <section id="exam-prep-insights">
    <h2>5. Strategic Insights for Exam Preparation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Plan around a stable MCQ base:</strong> every observed year contains exactly 15 MCQ rows, so rapid-recall practice should be a permanent preparation component rather than a year-specific adjustment.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not underweight Short questions:</strong> Short-question volume is the main source of structural variation and has remained at 15 rows in every observed year from 2021 to 2025.</li>
      <li><strong>Teach Long answers as synthesis tasks:</strong> Long questions are only 3 rows per observed year, but they repeatedly intersect with programming and database concepts; students need explanation, comparison, and multi-part response practice.</li>
      <li><strong>Prioritize programming fundamentals:</strong> terms such as <em>program</em>, <em>statement</em>, <em>loop</em>, <em>operator</em>, <em>printf</em>, and <em>syntax</em> recur frequently, indicating sustained assessment of C/C++ mechanics and output reasoning.</li>
      <li><strong>Maintain strong database coverage:</strong> <em>database</em>, <em>data</em>, <em>key</em>, <em>table</em>, <em>primary key</em>, <em>foreign key</em>, and <em>relational database</em> are prominent; curriculum pacing should reserve repeated practice time for these concepts.</li>
      <li><strong>Treat smaller topic areas as targeted review:</strong> web/internet, object-oriented, data-structure, operating-system, and number-system signals appear, but at much lower frequencies than programming and databases in this dataset.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="conclusions">
    <h2>6. Conclusions & Limitations</h2>
    <p><strong>Answer to the central question:</strong> the exam structure is partly stable and partly shifted. MCQs and Long questions are highly stable in row count, but Short questions changed meaningfully across periods: 9 rows in 2015–2016, 18 rows in 2017–2019, and 15 rows in 2021–2025. Educators should therefore treat the exam as structurally stable in its fixed components, while remaining responsive to Short-question workload changes.</p>
    <h3>Limitations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Missing 2020 data:</strong> data.csv contains no 2020 rows, so no 2020 statistics or trends are inferred.</li>
      <li><strong>No annual/supplementary flag:</strong> the dataset may mix annual and supplementary questions, but the CSV does not encode that distinction.</li>
      <li><strong>Keyword-based topic detection:</strong> topic patterns are derived from text frequencies and curated keyword groups, so they should be read as signals rather than official syllabus labels.</li>
      <li><strong>Community-compiled source:</strong> the dataset is compact and may contain transcription inconsistencies, although no missing values are present in the core columns.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">Report generated from <code>/app/benchmark/data.csv</code>. No external figures or fabricated values were used.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="footer">Prepared for educators and curriculum planners reviewing BIEK HSC Part-2 Computer Science Paper-II trends.</div>
</main>
